---
// Article (doc 74 §5C). Rewrite of "Results not typical" (2008). The ONLY
// place the Bonza-era before/after photos appear on the site, and always with
// "results vary from person to person" beside them. Numbers: 2009, eighteen
// months, month three, five years (four).
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../data/site';

import results1 from '../../assets/articles/results-1.jpg';
import results2 from '../../assets/articles/results-2.jpg';

const title = 'Results Not Typical: the Honest Way to Read a Before-and-After';
const description =
  'What the before-and-after photo shows, what it leaves out, and what a slower result is worth. Jamie Atlas, Denver.';
const published = '2026-09-10';
const url = `${SITE.url}/resources/results-not-typical`;

const articleSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: title,
  description,
  url,
  datePublished: published,
  dateModified: published,
  author: { '@id': `${SITE.url}/#jamie` },
  publisher: { '@id': SITE.url },
  mainEntityOfPage: { '@type': 'WebPage', '@id': url },
};
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(articleSchema)} />

  <section class="px-6 pt-16 pb-12 md:pt-24">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <p class="font-serif italic text-[var(--color-primary)] mb-3 text-lg">
        <a href="/resources" class="no-underline">Resources</a> · Expectations
      </p>
      <h1 class="mb-8">Results not typical: the honest way to read a before-and-after</h1>
      <p class="text-lg md:text-xl text-[var(--color-text-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        "Results not typical." For years that line sat under every transformation photo in the fitness industry, in
        type small enough to deny. In 2009 the Federal Trade Commission changed its endorsement guides so that a
        fine-print disclaimer no longer got you off the hook: if you show an unusual result, you have to say what a
        typical one looks like.<sup><a href="#s1">1</a></sup> Most of the industry responded by making the photos
        bigger.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="px-6 py-14 bg-[var(--color-surface)] border-y border-[var(--color-border)]">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto space-y-6 text-lg text-[var(--color-text-muted)] leading-relaxed">
      <h2 class="mb-2">What the photo shows</h2>
      <p>
        One person, on their best day, in their best light, at the end. Usually the person who responded fastest, out
        of everyone who started. Sometimes the person who was paid.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mb-2 pt-4">What it leaves out</h2>
      <p>
        Everyone else. The people who got a slower, quieter result and kept it. The people who got the fast result and
        lost it by the next photo. And the part nobody photographs: what happened in the eighteen months after.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-text)] font-semibold text-xl">A slow result you keep beats a fast one you rent.</p>

      <h2 class="mb-2 pt-4">How to read one</h2>
      <p>
        Ask what the typical result was, not the featured one. Ask how long ago the "after" was taken and whether it
        held. Ask whether the program built anything you could keep, like strength, or only removed something, like
        weight. And ask whether the person in the photo had a life like yours: a job, a knee, a family, a Tuesday.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mb-2 pt-4">My own photos</h2>
      <p>
        I have before-and-afters from the studio's Bonza Bodies years. They're real, the people consented, and they
        are the good outcomes, not the average ones. So they come with the line I'd want under anyone else's: results
        vary from person to person. What the average client here gets is slower, less photogenic, and still theirs
        five years later. That's the product.
      </p>
      <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 items-start">
        <figure class="m-0">
          <Image src={results1} alt="Two Bonza Bodies clients, Caitlin and Jessie, before and after their programs" widths={[435]} sizes="(min-width: 640px) 50vw, 100vw" format="webp" loading="lazy" class="rounded-lg border border-[var(--color-border)] w-full h-auto" />
          <figcaption class="mt-2 text-sm">Results vary from person to person.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="m-0">
          <Image src={results2} alt="A Bonza Bodies client before and after her program, hiking and at home" widths={[735]} sizes="(min-width: 640px) 50vw, 100vw" format="webp" loading="lazy" class="rounded-lg border border-[var(--color-border)] w-full h-auto" />
          <figcaption class="mt-2 text-sm">Results vary from person to person.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <h2 class="mb-2 pt-4">The honest frame</h2>
      <p>
        Expect the first month to feel like nothing. Expect month three to feel like something. Expect the photo to
        happen, if it happens, around the time you've stopped caring about it. That's not a sales pitch. It's the
        timeline I watch play out, one client at a time, in a basement on Glenarm Place.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-text)] font-semibold">
        Bottom line: the box never worked. The person who did the work every Tuesday did.
      </p>
      <p class="text-base">
        <a href="/resources/why-the-scale-lies">Why the scale lies →</a> ·
        <a href="/resources/why-weight-loss-stalls">Why weight loss stalls →</a> ·
        <a href="/personal-trainer-denver/glp-1">Training on a GLP-1 →</a>
      </p>

      <p class="text-sm pt-4" id="s1">
        1. Federal Trade Commission, Guides Concerning the Use of Endorsements and Testimonials in Advertising, 16 CFR
        Part 255, revised 2009.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="px-6 py-20 bg-[var(--color-primary)] text-on-dark">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto text-center">
      <h2 class="mb-6 text-white">The typical result starts with thirty minutes.</h2>
      <p class="text-xl mb-10 opacity-95">
        A 30-minute assessment, {SITE.pricing.firstVisit}: your history, my plain read on whether I can help, and one change you can measure before you leave.
      </p>
      <a href="/book" class="cta-button cta-button-accent text-lg">Book a 30-minute assessment</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
